{"agent_id":"OL2r","agent_seq":703,"timestamp":"2026-08-04T00:47:45.128557079Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"0d8a410c-743f-4602-8c96-9863d1b1b384","comment_uuid":"7a183abd-e725-4956-a57b-11e666f59f07","author":"OL2r","content":"SCOPE CORRECTION (2026-08-04, operator + orchestrator): this convention is TOOLING, not methodology — it's 'how project work is done', and not every project needs it (single-site projects won't have multi-site base-path concerns). DO NOT add a full section to the ASES playbook that every project inherits. REVISED: (1) put the convention in the TOOLS toolkit (#164) as a knowledge page + reusable helper (#170); (2) projects that need it (multi-site) reference it from their own docs/tooling config; (3) at most a one-line pointer in the playbook tooling section, not a full convention section. Mirrors the tooling/methodology boundary (#162).","created_at":"2026-08-04T00:47:45.122736022Z","kind":"observation","signed_by":"SHA256:txtxRbCGncxPs5YVqhd0abUgfcruU3ze6o9USZ9e9ck","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgBrkJWbwQmXES5+ltWRxRKeJmS2PIO3BfyCG5CwZdt0oAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABATX9jpixO1eSDie7c5SM6HnIRgEtKWIRmemwBtLlxZ1pV7ENe9XIZZxlHPaXHNHYfxa7i9vqtL4nOx35M3Dj8DA=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:txtxRbCGncxPs5YVqhd0abUgfcruU3ze6o9USZ9e9ck","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgBrkJWbwQmXES5+ltWRxRKeJmS2PIO3BfyCG5CwZdt0oAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQEIa6mOvq2uz8tRbwBwlou1NeutXfp6jQi8vYN7fuI1ML2pFEFbaYNsqYijLPwEp2TKYarrvxJAdFgsqA/0xtQo="}
{"agent_id":"OL2r","agent_seq":704,"timestamp":"2026-08-04T00:47:58.478185664Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"938b926a-c400-4088-837f-9bc1322dd8d7","title":"DESIGN: orchestrator markdown notes via crosslink, no filesystem write needed. MOTIVATION (operator): the orchestrator is often the best-placed agent for retrospectives, handoff notes, and housekeeping markdown — but it has NO filesystem write (edit denied, orchestrator-guard blocks write tools for non-Builder). KEY INSIGHT: crosslink ALREADY provides a markdown-write path the orchestrator can use — 'crosslink knowledge add/edit' (knowledge pages, in the orchestrator allowlist), 'crosslink issue comment' (markdown comments), 'crosslink session end --notes' (handoff notes). DESIGN QUESTION: standardize on crosslink-managed markdown (knowledge pages + issue comments + session notes) as the orchestrator's note surface, rather than granting scoped filesystem write — keeps the write path narrow, auditable, sync-aware, and permission-compatible. OPTIONS: (A) use existing crosslink knowledge/comment/notes as-is (zero code); (B) add a narrow 'crosslink note' command that writes a dated note file via crosslink itself (crosslink does the file write on the orchestrator's behalf, tool surface unchanged); (C) grant scoped *.md-only filesystem write to orchestrator (guard carve-out — riskier, NOT recommended). Recommend evaluating A first (likely sufficient), B if repo-file notes are truly needed. Deliverable: design assessment + recommendation. Research/design task — flash model. Related: #162 (tooling/methodology boundary), #168 (retrospective intake), #164 (Tools migration).","priority":"medium","created_by":"OL2r"},"signed_by":"SHA256:txtxRbCGncxPs5YVqhd0abUgfcruU3ze6o9USZ9e9ck","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgBrkJWbwQmXES5+ltWRxRKeJmS2PIO3BfyCG5CwZdt0oAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQEQEvmiWifayjgwx0rl1INeAEvF3ftGpNDJ3X3hMv2ViUP55OeGwEwses6+AX08YV