{"agent_id":"OL2r","agent_seq":824,"timestamp":"2026-08-05T23:21:33.348321333Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"036319c2-0184-46ff-9ab1-7eeb54c44e65","comment_uuid":"81796bc7-885c-4dcb-8ee5-caf0fbecf4e5","author":"OL2r","content":"# RE-REVIEW VERDICT r1 (#182) — REVISED #154 design on #180, round 2\n\n**Verdict: CONDITIONAL** — approve for implementation *after* five specific corrections (V1–V5). Do not start the build against §7 as written.\n\n**Reviewer**: Builder pp3g-CGdk (hy3), independent. **Isolation**: I did not read #176, #177, #178, or any other re-review sub-issue. Inputs read: #180 (revised design), #175, #144, #145, #154.\n\n**Method**: every checkable claim was re-derived from primary sources, not from §8's change-log — a real upstream checkout at `v1.18.13` (`/tmp/opencode/opencode-upstream-r3`, `git describe` = `v1.18.13`), the live installed binary, and the live config. ~20 source citations were re-verified independently.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Independent verification of the design's factual base\n\nThe revision's source accuracy is high. Everything below I confirmed myself:\n\n| Design claim | Independent check | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `provider.ts` `wrapSSE` L37–82, guard L40 | `grep -n \"text/event-stream\"` → `40:` | ✔ |\n| `packages/core/src/aisdk.ts` `wrapSSE` L26–72, guard L29 | → `29:` | ✔ |\n| v2 copy raises plain `Error`, no `headerTimeout` | read `aisdk.ts:26–72`, `75–119` | ✔ |\n| `resolveSDK` fetch wrapper ~L1738–1767 | read `provider.ts:1737–1767` | ✔ |\n| `provider.ts:1746` = per-call `new AbortController()`, only SDK memoised | line 1746 exactly; `s.sdk.get(key)` at 1734–35 | ✔ (R3 downgrade is correct) |\n| `meta.attempt` is a 1-based **retry** count | upstream `retry.test.ts:89–115` — two `step(error)` calls ⇒ `attempt: 2` | ✔ |\n| `policy()` unbounded, `Cause.done(meta.attempt)` at 185; `delay = 2000·2^(a−1)` | `retry.ts:176–199`, `:26,61` | ✔ |\n| retry-after honoured (ms / secs / HTTP-date), capped at `RETRY_MAX_DELAY` | `retry.ts:34–66` | ✔ |\n| codes `ProviderResponseStreamError` / `ProviderHeaderTimeoutError` / `ECONNRESET` | `provider/error.ts:8,16`; `message-v2.ts:625,653,665` (`code: e.name`) | ✔ |\n| `halt()` at `processor.ts:599` | ✔ | ✔ |\n| `llm.ts` 271 / 283 / 323 / 373 (`fullStream`) | all four exact | ✔ |\n| `build.ts:192–199` defines `OPENCODE_VERSION`/`OPENCODE_CHANNEL`; `--skip-embed-web-ui` | `define` block; flag at `build.ts:24` | ✔ |\n| `InstallationVersion = OPENCODE_VERSION` | `core/src/installation/version.ts:6`; printed at `cli/cmd/debug/index.ts:61` | ✔ |\n| root `packageManager: bun@1.3.14`, root `postinstall: fix-node-pty` | root `package.json` | ✔ |\n| §0 live state | binary `1.18.13`, mtime `2026-08-05 22:47`, size `180,381,824`; `autoupdate:false`; both providers `chunkTimeout/headerTimeout=300000`, `timeout=3600000`, no `bodyIdleTimeout` | ✔ exact |\n| exactly **two** guard copies, both byte-patched | 2 × `if(false)return` (`…$;` and `…o;`); the 3rd `\"SSE read timed out\"` hit is a TUI string-match (`tui/src/feature-plugins/system/notifications.ts:23`), not a guard | ✔ |\n\n§0 is the most accurate section in the document — byte-exact. The change-log did not need to be trusted anywhere.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Verdict per round-1 item\n\n| Item | Status | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| **B1** both guard copies | **PARTIAL** | Decision + reachability evidence closed; **Patch B does not compile** → V1 |\n| **B2** Option A' + numeric chain | **PARTIAL** | Shape adopted & Option-B false-abort class documented; **numeric chain not established** → V4 |\n| **B3** class-scoped budget, off-by-one | **PARTIAL** | Arithmetic correct (≤3 POSTs); terminal-WARN payload reintroduces the off-by-one → V5; unbounded class admits `ZlibError` → F-new-3 |\n| **F4** per-attempt WARN + grep-assert | **PARTIAL** | Per-attempt WARN sound & correctly located; terminal WARN specified on a non-Effect return path → V5 |\n| **F5** parsed-stream guard | **SUBSTANTIALLY CLOSED** | Best section; guard left \"(recommended)\" though it is the only bound on the production signature → F-new-5 |\n| **F6** pull() shape | **CLOSED** | Mirrors upstream `wrapSSE` exactly; backpressure preserved |\n| **F7** timer/controller lifecycle | **PARTIAL** | `settled`/`clearTimeout`/`.catch` closed for the idle path; the **new absolute-cap timer is unsound** → V3 |\n| **F8** opt-in | **CLOSED** (+2 new) | Opt-in semantics + absent-content-type correct; header passthrough undecided and config schema untouched → F-new-1/2 |\n| **F9** POST-count invariant | **CLOSED** | `input.retries ?? 0` verified at `llm.ts:323` |\n| **F10** atomic install | **CLOSED** | stage + same-fs `mv`; swap target verified sane (nvm `bin/opencode` → `opencode.exe` symlink) |\n| **F11** build prereqs | **CLOSED** (substance) | Root install / bun pin / web-UI decision all correct; **citation wrong** → F-new-4 |\n| **F12** fork marker + drift | **CLOSED** | Mechanism verified end-to-end; two minor unexamined interactions → F-new-6/7 |\n| **F13** step-0 stopgap | **CLOSED** | Independently confirmed byte-exact |\n| **R1** budget predicate pinned | **CLOSED** | See B3 |\n| **R2** test-6 gaps > chunkTimeout | **NOT CLOSED** | Fixed literally, broken substantively — recurs across the matrix → **V2** |\n| **R3** PER_REQUEST mode | **CLOSED** | Source-confirmed downgrade verified at `provider.ts:1746` |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Conditions (must fix before implementation)\n\n### V1 — MAJOR · B1: Patch B (`aisdk.ts`, hunk 4) is not implementable as written\n\nTwo distinct defects in the v2 dispatcher diff:\n\n1. Line `if (!bodyIdleAbortCtl || typeof bodyIdleTimeout !== \"number\") return res` references **`bodyIdleAbortCtl`, which the hunk never declares.** Hunk 2 (`provider.ts`) *does* declare its equivalent; hunk 4 does not. The v2 patch does not compile.\n2. The hunk pushes a second `new AbortController()` into the `signals` array but leaves the selector unchanged:\n   ```ts\n   const chunkAbortCtl = signals.find((item): item is AbortController => item instanceof AbortController)\n   ```\n   (verified verbatim at `aisdk.ts:95`). `find` returns the **first** controller. With `chunkTimeout` unset and `bodyIdleTimeout` set, `chunkAbortCtl` silently binds to the *body* controller. Positional-`find` controller selection cannot survive a second controller.\n\nThe design says \"the patch text must be finalised against the exact source at implementation\" — but that note does not name either defect, and B1 was a blocker specifically about the second copy. **Required**: replace the `signals.find(...)` selection with explicitly-named `chunkAbortCtl` / `bodyIdleAbortCtl` bindings (as hunk 2 already does) and re-state hunk 4 against `v1.18.13`.\n\n*Sub-minor:* the file is `packages/core/src/aisdk.ts`. The design writes **`aiskd.ts`** in §2.1, the §2.3 error-class note, and — the one that matters — the §6.5 **mandatory rebase checklist** (\"`provider.ts` + `aiskd.ts` guard count = 0 each\"). A mandatory checklist naming a nonexistent path will pass vacuously.\n\n### V2 — MAJOR · R2 not closed: §7 harness parameters make the new mechanism unobservable\n\n§7.1 keeps the harness at `timeout: 15000` and adds `bodyIdleTimeout: 30000`. **The new deadline is larger than the request timeout**, so `wrapBodyIdle` can never fire in the harness. This is not speculative: #145 established that the request-level `AbortSignal.timeout` *does* fire on a stalled body (\"the 15 s 'operation timed out' … is the `AbortSignal.timeout` overall timeout firing\"), correcting #144 — and §5.1 of this very design depends on that correction. The matrix contradicts its own §5.1.\n\nConsequences, row by row:\n\n- **Test 1** (`application/json` + hang, expect \"abort within `bodyIdleTimeout`\") — aborts at 15 s via the *pre-existing* request timeout. Measures the old mechanism; cannot pass or fail the new one.\n- **Test 6 is guaranteed to fail as specified.** `SLOW_CHUNK_MS=5000` × 5 chunks = 20–25 s end-to-end against `timeout: 15000`. The single test written to prove hy3 **F2** (\"slow-but-legitimate non-SSE completes, EXIT 0, no abort\") will false-abort at 15 s for a reason unrelated to the change. R2 asked for gaps > `chunkTimeout` (5 s > 3 s ✔) — the revision fixed that pair and left the total-vs-`timeout` pair broken. Same defect class, one parameter to the left.\n- **Test 6b** — \"aborts at `bodyIdleTimeout` (≤ ~30 s)\" is satisfied at 15 s for the wrong reason. Confounded.\n- **Test 9 is unrunnable**: \"chunks every 15 min, `bodyIdleTimeout=600000`\" against `timeout: 15000` dies at 15 s; no cfg override and no ≥10-min wall-clock budget is specified — for the row the design itself calls the A'-vs-B discriminator.\n- **Test 8** (2000 vs 4000 vs `timeout` 15000) is the one correctly-parameterised deadline row, which shows the right pattern was available.\n\n**Required**: state a self-consistent cfg with `headerTimeout < chunkTimeout < bodyIdleTimeout < timeout` and a slow-body total inside `timeout` — e.g. `headerTimeout=2000`, `chunkTimeout=3000`, `bodyIdleTimeout=6000`, `timeout=60000`, test 6 = 5 chunks × 5 s gaps (25 s < 60 s), test 9 rescaled to 15 s gaps with `bodyIdleTimeout=10000`. Then re-derive every \"abort within X\" expectation against it.\n\n### V3 — MAJOR · F7 not closed: the absolute-cap path cannot terminate the stream\n\nThe `settled` guard, `clearTimeout` on both read outcomes, and `.catch(()=>{})` on the cancel promise are all correct — the **idle** path is sound because it owns and `reject`s its own promise. The newly-introduced `bodyAbsoluteTimeout` timer is not:\n\n1. **No termination path.** The absolute callback has no reference to the stream `ctrl`, so it can neither `close()` nor `error()` the stream. It calls `ctl.abort(err)` then `reader.cancel(err)`. Per WHATWG, `cancel()` *fulfils* pending read requests with `{done:true}` — it does not reject. If that fulfilment wins the race against the fetch abort erroring the body, the awaited promise **resolves**, `pull` hits `if (settled) return`, and the controller is never closed or errored. The consumer's read never settles, and `pull` may be re-invoked into a no-op loop. That is a re-creation of the silent-hang class *inside the fix*, on the path documented as \"belt-and-braces\".\n2. **Timer leak.** The idle-timeout callback sets `settled = true` but never clears `absoluteId`; `cancel()` early-returns on `if (settled) return` **before** its `clearTimeout(absoluteId)`. Only the `part.done` branch clears it. After an idle abort the absolute timer stays armed for up to 55 min at the proposed default.\n\n**Required**: give the absolute timer a reference to the stream controller (or a shared `fail(err)` helper that both timers call) so it errors the stream deterministically; clear `absoluteId` in the idle-timeout callback; move `clearTimeout(absoluteId)` in `cancel()` above the `settled` early-return.\n\n### V4 — MODERATE · B2: the numeric chain is asserted, not established\n\nThe shape adjudication is right and §3.2's critique of #175's mis-scoring is correct. The numbers are not:\n\n- §3.3 compares `bodyIdleTimeout × POSTs` (30 min, a **chain total**) against the request `timeout` (60 min, a **per-request** bound). Those are different quantities; the comparison is not the safety property it is presented as. The genuinely meaningful per-request property (10 min idle < 60 min timeout) does hold and is stated separately in bullet 4.\n- The `< operator timeout` leg **fails against this project's own documented ladder**. `SESSION-START.md` §4a fixes task-matched ceilings at trivial ≤10 m, doc/review 15–20 m, port 30 m. These very review agents were launched on 20 m. A 30-min worst-case detection latency means the agent is killed *before* any ERROR surfaces — from the operator's seat the silent-hang symptom is unchanged for every short-timeout task. §3.3 concedes this in a parenthetical (\"for shorter agents the operator timeout fires first as the outer net\") and then does not resolve it.\n- Unjustified asymmetry: production `chunkTimeout=300000` already yields 15-min SSE detection. Choosing `bodyIdleTimeout=600000` makes non-SSE detection 2× *slower* on qualitative grounds only.\n\n**Required**: either set `bodyIdleTimeout=300000` (⇒ 15-min worst case, parity with SSE, fits the 15–20 m ladder), or state the rule that binds `bodyIdleTimeout × (RETRY_MAX_RETRIES+1)` below the *smallest* operator timeout in use and record the exception for shorter tasks.\n\n### V5 — MODERATE · B3/F4: the terminal WARN reintroduces the off-by-one and sits on a non-Effect path\n\n1. **Arithmetic.** §4.2.3 is right: `meta.attempt` 1-based, stop at `> 2` ⇒ exactly 3 POSTs (I confirmed the semantics against upstream `retry.test.ts`). But §4.2.1's terminal WARN payload is `total POSTs: meta.attempt + 1`. At the stop point `meta.attempt = 3` and total POSTs = 3, so the log reports **4**. The exact off-by-one B3/R1 demanded be fixed is reintroduced in the observability field that §4.2.3 and test 14 elevate to *the* invariant — the operator-visible number will contradict the server-side counter.\n2. **Placement.** The terminal WARN is specified on the `Cause.done` path, but in source that path is a synchronous, non-Effect `return Cause.done(meta.attempt)` (`retry.ts:185`). `yield* Effect.logWarning` cannot be placed there without restructuring the step's return. The design shows no restructuring. (The per-attempt WARN is fine — it lands inside the existing `Effect.gen` at `retry.ts:187–197`, where `wait`/`now` are already in scope.)\n\n**Required**: `total POSTs: meta.attempt`, and show the restructured `Cause.done` branch.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Additional findings (not blocking, should be recorded)\n\n- **F-new-1 (Moderate) — `ZlibError` sits in the unbounded retry class.** `message-v2.ts` maps decompression failure to `APIError{ isRetryable: true, code: \"ZlibError\" }`. It is *not* in the bounded class {`ProviderResponseStreamError`, `ProviderHeaderTimeoutError`, `ECONNRESET`}. It is also the failure most likely to be *introduced* by `wrapBodyIdle` re-wrapping a compressed body — the design's own test-10 concern. A deterministic decode failure in the unbounded class = infinite retry at the 30 s `RETRY_MAX_DELAY_NO_HEADERS` cap: a fresh instance of the exact failure mode this work exists to eliminate. F4's per-attempt WARN makes it noisy rather than silent, which is the mitigation — but `ZlibError` belongs in the bounded class.\n- **F-new-2 (Moderate) — header passthrough on the synthesized Response is undecided.** `wrapBodyIdle` copies `new Headers(res.headers)` verbatim, retaining `Content-Encoding`/`Content-Length` that no longer describe the re-chunked stream. Upstream `wrapSSE` does the same, but SSE is rarely compressed while provider JSON commonly is, so the risk profile differs materially — and the resulting failure lands in the unbounded class (F-new-1). The design adds an empirical test (10) but takes no design decision. Either strip `content-encoding`/`content-length`, or state with a Bun-behaviour citation why passthrough is safe.\n- **F-new-3 (Moderate) — no config-schema change.** `packages/core/src/v1/config/provider.ts:96–121` declares `timeout`/`headerTimeout`/`chunkTimeout` with descriptions, then extends with `[Schema.Record(Schema.String, Schema.Any)]`. So `bodyIdleTimeout` will be accepted — **and so will `bodyIdletimeout`, silently, with no validation error and no editor completion**. For a knob whose entire purpose is hang protection, silent typo tolerance re-creates \"protection silently absent\". The design never mentions the schema (nor `packages/sdk/openapi.json`). Applies equally to `bodyAbsoluteTimeout` and `experimental.llmIdleTimeout`.\n- **F-new-4 (Minor) — F11 citation is wrong.** The bun gate is not in `packages/opencode/script/script.ts` (no such file). It is `packages/script/src/index.ts:5–18`, imported as `@opencode-ai/script`, and the range is `^${rootPkg.packageManager.split(\"@\")[1]}` = **`^1.3.14`**, a caret range, not an exact pin. Substance right, citation unfollowable.\n- **F-new-5 (Moderate) — F5's guard is \"(recommended)\" while its instrumentation is \"(mandatory)\".** By the design's own §5.1, the turn-level guard is the *only* mechanism that bounds the actual production signature (#142's 10.9 h). Leaving it optional lets an implementer ship §2–§4, pass the whole raw-body matrix, and still reproduce the original hang. Make it mandatory or state an explicit gate.\n- **F-new-6 (Minor) — `OPENCODE_CHANNEL=fork-pp3g` makes `IS_PREVIEW` true** (`packages/script/src/index.ts:31`), and `1.18.13-pp3g-fork` is a semver *prerelease* sorting below `1.18.13`, which `cli/upgrade.ts:28` (`Installation.getReleaseType`) will compare against npm latest. Inert under `autoupdate:false`, but §6.4 depends on `--version` semantics and never examines the upgrade-path interaction.\n- **F-new-7 (Minor) — drift-check cost.** `~/.local/bin/opencode` already spawns `\"$REAL\" models` on every launch (verified). §6.4 adds a second `\"$REAL\" --version` spawn before every agent launch. Acknowledge the cost or fold the assertion into the existing `models` call.\n- **F-new-8 (Minor) — new error messages lose a UI mapping.** `tui/src/feature-plugins/system/notifications.ts:23` matches `data.message === \"SSE read timed out\"` exactly to render \"Model stopped responding\". \"Body read timed out\" / \"Turn stalled…\" will fall through to \"Session error\". Off our `opencode run` path; informational.\n- **F-new-9 (Minor) — matrix gaps.** (a) §7.3 item 5 names no file for the `wrapBodyIdle` unit tests; the existing `packages/opencode/test/provider/header-timeout.test.ts:49` already has `it.live(\"chunkTimeout raises a response stream error when SSE body stalls\")` with a live HTTP fixture — a directly reusable template, and it would make item 5 as concrete as items 1–4. (b) **No row exercises the v2 `aisdk.ts` patch at all.** B1 forced a two-copy patch; the matrix verifies one copy. If §2.1's unreachability conclusion makes a runtime test impossible, promote the §6.5 static grep-counts into §7 as the explicit acceptance criterion for Patch B rather than leaving them only in the rebase checklist.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Why CONDITIONAL and not PASS or FAIL\n\n**Not FAIL.** The revision does real work. B1's decision is made and evidenced; F5's causal argument (a 1 h `AbortSignal` cannot produce a 10.9 h hang, therefore the stall is above the raw-body layer) is correct and is the most valuable contribution in either round; F6, F9, F10, F12, F13, R1, R3 are cleanly closed; F8's opt-in semantics are right; F11 is right in substance. The source scholarship is unusually good — I re-derived ~20 citations against a real `v1.18.13` tree and the live binary and found the substance accurate everywhere, with §0 byte-exact.\n\n**Not PASS.** Three defects block implementation as written: the v2 patch does not compile and carries an unflagged controller-aliasing hazard (V1); the verification matrix's own parameters make the new mechanism unobservable and guarantee that the one test proving F2-preservation fails (V2); and the absolute-cap path has no deterministic way to terminate the stream, re-creating the hang class inside the fix (V3). V4 and V5 mean two of the three round-1 blockers — the numeric chain and the off-by-one — are closed in prose but not in the numbers the design itself nominates as the invariants.\n\nThe common thread is that the *decisions* were re-derived carefully and the *arithmetic* was not. V1–V5 are all mechanical, locally-scoped corrections; none requires re-opening an adjudication. Re-issue §2.3 hunk 4, §3.3, §4.2.1, the `wrapBodyIdle` absolute-cap lifecycle, and the §7.1 cfg, and this reaches PASS without another design round.","created_at":"2026-08-05T23:21:33.340348439Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:txtxRbCGncxPs5YVqhd0abUgfcruU3ze6o9USZ9e9ck","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgBrkJWbwQmXES5+ltWRxRKeJmS2PIO3BfyCG5CwZdt0oAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAmdCL3AyPzafaFiO4Vu+JRGxtcydPsR/ka7Rc3ojFvzx5YB/r6RfvLYn6Y6viGa3n0BtcQvEQlJCLZVBfujRxBg=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:txtxRbCGncxPs5YVqhd0abUgfcruU3ze6o9USZ9e9ck","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgBrkJWbwQmXES5+ltWRxRKeJmS2PIO3BfyCG5CwZdt0oAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQCtDWh3PQr1x2XF+5FY4Wew8/2hNzLM1IgZuLd2Bp4yKJinHkYZWa3EFul6k9HOUDu562/qEK8Q0ROx+WQMoSgw="}
{"agent_id":"OL2r","agent_seq":825,"timestamp":"2026-08-05T23:21:42.999953084Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"51942b54-b189-47ee-a0ad-be20dd4840f0","title":"LATER/DEFERRED (separate session): surface agent-completion updates IN the opencode orchestrator chat. OPERATOR REQUEST (2026-08-05, session #13): when a kickoff/swarm agent completes, the operator wants the update to appear in the opencode chat as a second orchestrator message — WITHOUT polling ('status?') and WITHOUT desktop/webhook notifications. RESEARCH FINDING (verified against @opencode-ai/plugin + SDK types): (1) plugin 'event' hook receives ALL SDK events incl. 'session.idle' (fires when the model finishes a turn — the trigger point); (2) /session/{id}/prompt_async lets a plugin inject a prompt into a session asynchronously — the injected prompt appears as a new user turn ('update acting as prompt from the operator'); (3) EventTuiToastShow exists for TUI toasts. PROPOSED DESIGN: small plugin in the orchestrator session (alongside crosslink-guard) hooks session.idle -> checks crosslink / .worktrees/*/.kickoff-status for transitions -> on completion injects via prompt_async 'Agent X completed - report status'; orchestrator reads hub and sends the operator a second message. PRECEDENT: #133/#134 surveyed community plugins (@mohak34/opencode-notifier, opencode-notificator, opencode-notify) — all observe OpenCode session events only, none watch crosslink/tmux kickoff agents nor inject prompts back; none fit. #135 Phase 1 watcher (kickoff-notify.py) covers desktop/webhook only. FILE NO DISPATCH — prototype in a separate session, prove against the live orchestrator session.","priority":"low","created_by":"OL2r"},"signed_by":"SHA256:txtxRbCGncxPs5YVqhd0abUgfcruU3ze6o9USZ9e9ck","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgBrkJWbwQmXES5+ltWRxRKeJmS2PIO3BfyCG5CwZdt0oAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQK/hKwdP+/YghVnmEP6tqaTmlDbrkoZSTawnTLC9gfs45Dj9N0HYnMhcJ