{"agent_id":"OL2r","agent_seq":892,"timestamp":"2026-08-06T01:47:02.345801183Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"6d758d74-81d2-4621-8005-64e83425d269","title":"MEASURE: webfetch (built-in) vs fetch MCP — token efficiency + content completeness on the SAME URLs. MOTIVATION: the fetch-vs-curl comparison (#187) showed 9-24x savings, but agents don't use curl for web content — they use the built-in webfetch tool, which ALREADY returns markdown (TurndownService, source verified: packages/opencode/src/tool/webfetch.ts — format default=markdown, strips script/style/meta/link, 5MB cap, no default truncation). fetch MCP (mcp-server-fetch) returns markdown too but with a DEFAULT 5000-char cap. So the real question is: does fetch MCP's cap beat webfetch's full-length markdown on tokens, and at what content cost? TASK: (1) on 3-5 real URLs (a small docs page, a large docs page, a blog post, a news page — e.g. opencode.ai/docs, opencode.ai/docs/cli/, a README, a Wikipedia article), fetch the same URL via (a) webfetch tool output (markdown) and (b) fetch MCP output; (2) measure input tokens (tiktoken o200k_base) for each; (3) measure content completeness (total chars, whether key sections present, truncation evidence); (4) report a table: URL | webfetch tokens | fetch-MCP tokens | ratio | content-completeness diff; (5) VERDICT: does fetch MCP save enough tokens vs webfetch to justify routing agents to it, or is webfetch the right default? Note the tradeoff: fetch's 5k cap bounds tokens but loses content; webfetch returns full markdown (possibly 40k+ tokens on huge pages) with no cap. Also test webfetch with an explicit format=text option if relevant. Read /tmp/opencode/opencode-upstream-r3/packages/opencode/src/tool/webfetch.ts for the webfetch semantics. POST result to THIS sub-issue.","priority":"medium","created_by":"OL2r"},"signed_by":"SHA256:txtxRbCGncxPs5YVqhd0abUgfcruU3ze6o9USZ9e9ck","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgBrkJWbwQmXES5+ltWRxRKeJmS2P