{"agent_id":"OL2r","agent_seq":1119,"timestamp":"2026-08-07T22:28:07.694713444Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"a6aa399a-7e42-409c-bd0b-d4574239b93c","title":"REVIEW r4 (Gemini 3.5, architectural-audit lens) — WORKFLOW-TOPOLOGY PROPOSAL review. Review the proposal for adding review/evidence stops to the agent workflow. THE PROPOSAL (concise statement to review): (1) TWO PRINCIPLES: (a) reasoning certainty — claims crossing a role boundary must state WHY, WHAT they're based on, and HOW CERTAIN (guess/evidence-based/proven), including what was NOT tested; (b) cheapest-test-first — assumptions that gate decisions must be tested with the quickest cheapest discriminating test. (2) TOPOLOGY FINDING: in role-separated agent systems, decision-gating claims crossing an information-asymmetry boundary (producer can verify cheaply, thin consumer cannot) are the dominant unguarded failure point; the corrective is producer-side calibration + cheapest-test obligation. (3) WORKFLOW STOPS: STOP A (post-plan testability audit, L1 cheap), STOP B (pre-consumption result audit — the load-bearing gate at the producer-consumer edge), STOP C (targeted L2 re-verification, only for high-stakes decisions like install/merge). (4) MODEL-AGREEMENT COLLECTION: agreement data recorded as a zero-cost byproduct of the standing multi-model review workflow (#241). ASSESS from the ARCHITECTURAL/AUDIT lens: is the proposal internally consistent as a methodology? Does it respect the EDASES research / ASES methodology / execution-engine implementation layering? Is the thin-operator + thin-orchestrator design preserved? Are the principles (certainty labeling, cheapest-test) the right abstraction level for the registry/orchestration separation? What is the risk of the proposal becoming bureaucracy or cargo-cult verification? Verdict: PASS / CONDITIONAL / FAIL with specific findings. POST to THIS sub-issue. Do NOT read other reviewer sub-issues.","priority":"medium","created_by":"OL2r"},"signed_by":"