{"agent_id":"OL2r","agent_seq":1147,"timestamp":"2026-08-07T22:40:42.449966382Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"b680ab23-cfe8-497f-949d-6b10e0d5205e","comment_uuid":"24e8429b-55fb-4ccf-a400-b569f2151b9d","author":"OL2r","content":"[PROGRESS] state=working completed=plan posted; context grounded (3 orchestration tiers, adversarial-review pipeline, orchestrator contract, #241 schema §6, thin-orchestrator rule, reviewer-independence rule) next=conduct 6-axis assessment + adversarial counterexamples, draft verdict blocker=none","created_at":"2026-08-07T22:40:42.443749848Z","kind":"observation","signed_by":"SHA256:txtxRbCGncxPs5YVqhd0abUgfcruU3ze6o9USZ9e9ck","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgBrkJWbwQmXES5+ltWRxRKeJmS2PIO3BfyCG5CwZdt0oAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAx+w8CBej0cqH9YBzT6ZzvximS33qK6gNyR2k0NTiQ18CY8MlVxAD9FhilKeNZX6xK8U89x3PcE01tD2fvXjsAA=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:txtxRbCGncxPs5YVqhd0abUgfcruU3ze6o9USZ9e9ck","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgBrkJWbwQmXES5+ltWRxRKeJmS2PIO3BfyCG5CwZdt0oAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQFif+MLlXczE2J6akVMIhmxQE2q17y0rJSG9dMH2U9B4Gcl0sO0orlOhmyoAimspqLtfS5p/O/QNN/t8EocgOgc="}
{"agent_id":"OL2r","agent_seq":1148,"timestamp":"2026-08-07T22:41:26.323679274Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"b680ab23-cfe8-497f-949d-6b10e0d5205e","comment_uuid":"25adfa29-1d4e-4723-900a-4b8aa5ef6da1","author":"OL2r","content":"## VERDICT: CONDITIONAL\n\nIndependent breadth/adversarial review of the workflow-topology proposal (the concise statement in #244). No other reviewer sub-issues read (#242/#243/#245). Grounded in: the three orchestration tiers (kickoff/swarm/sentinel), the adversarial-review pipeline, the orchestrator contract (docs/ORCHESTRATOR.md), the agent-orchestration-playbook (§5.4 checkpoint contract, §5.6 reviewer independence, §5.7 thin-orchestrator rule), the #241 model-agreement schema §6 (collection process), and AGENTS.md principles.\n\nThe proposal's core insight — that decision-gating claims crossing an information-asymmetry boundary are the dominant unguarded failure point — is **correct and valuable**. The two principles (reasoning certainty, cheapest-test-first) are sound and adoptable. But the workflow stops have load-bearing gaps that must be hardened before implementation.\n\n---\n\n### FINDING 1 (CRITICAL) — STOP B has no defined performer (the self-audit paradox)\n\nSTOP B is \"the load-bearing gate at the producer-consumer edge\" — a pre-consumption result audit. The proposal never says WHO performs it. All three options fail:\n\n- **(a) Producer self-audits before submitting.** The producer reviewing its own certainty is the least reliable reviewer — it built the thing and has every incentive to overstate certainty. This violates AGENTS.md (\"The Builder does not review its own work\") and the playbook §1 (\"Reviewing your own work is rubber-stamping by default\"). So STOP B cannot be producer self-audit.\n- **(b) A separate auditor performs STOP B.** That is a new agent dispatch at every producer-consumer edge — contradicting \"L1 cheap.\" And the auditor has the SAME information-asymmetry problem: it is a thin consumer of the producer's work and cannot cheaply verify the producer's certainty claims. STOP B just moves the boundary one step back.\n- **(c) The consumer performs STOP B before consuming.** But the consumer (reviewer/orchestrator) is information-poor by design (thin-orchestrator rule §5.7; reviewer is read-only and cannot run the code). The consumer cannot audit what it cannot verify.\n\nThe proposal does not resolve which horn. A \"load-bearing gate\" with no defined gatekeeper is the single largest gap.\n\n### FINDING 2 (CRITICAL) — STOP B bounce-back is unbounded\n\nSTOP B audits the result; on failure the result \"bounces back\" to the producer for rework. The proposal specifies:\n- **no max-bounce count** — a producer and auditor with different standards can oscillate indefinitely;\n- **no escalation on repeated bounce** — the existing sentinel model has an exhaustion rule (Sonnet→Opus, max 2 attempts, then \"exhausted\"); STOP B has no analogue;\n- **no definition of \"audit pass\"** that is achievable in bounded rounds.\n\nConcrete adversarial loop: Builder submits → auditor says \"declare what you didn't test\" → Builder adds a not-tested section → auditor says \"it's incomplete, you missed X\" → Builder adds X → auditor says \"state your certainty for X\" → Builder adds \"evidence-based\" → auditor says \"your evidence (test Y) doesn't prove what you claim\" → … Each round is a full agent dispatch with no termination condition. The proposal needs: (a) a bounce cap, (b) an escalation path on exhaustion (→ STOP C, → operator), (c) a bounded \"audit pass\" criterion.\n\nThe bounce-back instruction is ITSELF a decision-gating claim crossing the consumer→producer boundary (in reverse). The proposal's own principle 1a (certainty calibration on claims crossing role boundaries) should apply to the bounce-back channel, but the proposal doesn't apply its own principle to its own control flow.\n\n### FINDING 3 (MAJOR) — L1/L2/L3 cost model is undefined and collides with existing vocabulary\n\nThe proposal uses \"L1 cheap\" (STOP A) and \"L2 re-verification\" (STOP C) without defining L1/L2/L3. The existing verification pipeline (crosslink-subagent-orchestration §Verification Pipeline) already defines `local`/`ci`/`thorough`. The proposal introduces a parallel L1/L2/L3 vocabulary without mapping to the existing tiers — a terminology collision that will cause routing ambiguity.\n\nTwo deeper problems:\n- **\"L1 cheap\" conflates reasoning cost with mechanical verification cost.** STOP A is a \"post-plan testability audit\" — a reasoning task (does the plan admit cheap discriminating tests?), not a mechanical check like `cargo check`. Calling an LLM reasoning audit \"L1 cheap\" misrepresents its cost. A testability audit by an LLM is not cheap in the same sense a compile check is cheap.\n- **STOP C \"targeted L2 re-verification, only for high-stakes decisions\"** leaves \"targeted\" and \"high-stakes\" undefined. Who classifies stake level? That classification is itself a decision-gating claim crossing an information-asymmetry boundary (the agent knows what's high-stakes; the orchestrator may not). The proposal doesn't define the stake-classification gate.\n\n### FINDING 4 (MAJOR) — Workflow-shape coverage is incomplete (sentinel, swarm inter-phase, orchestrator-as-consumer, operator edge)\n\nThe proposal frames the producer-consumer edge as Builder→Reviewer. It misses:\n\n- **Sentinel.** The sentinel is a persistent poll-triage-dispatch-collect loop with model escalation and NO human in the loop between dispatch and collect (the `agent-todo:*` label is the pre-dispatch human gate). STOP B (pre-consumption result audit) would need to run inside collect before the result is posted — but sentinel is autonomous. Adding STOP B either (a) makes sentinel non-autonomous (needs a human auditor), or (b) makes the agent self-audit its own result before posting — which is exactly the producer-self-audit the proposal's own topology finding warns against. The proposal is silent on sentinel.\n- **Swarm inter-phase edges.** In a swarm, phase N's output is consumed by phase N+1. The phase gate (`crosslink swarm gate`) runs the test suite — a verification gate, not a certainty/calibration gate. The proposal's STOP B is singular (\"the producer-consumer edge\"); a swarm has N such edges (every phase boundary, every agent→hub sync, hub→orchestrator). Applying STOP B at every edge contradicts \"L1 cheap\"; applying it only at the final edge misses inter-phase contamination. The proposal doesn't address this multiplicity.\n- **Orchestrator-as-consumer.** The orchestrator is the central consumer of ALL agent outputs (it reads verdicts, synthesizes, decides merge/dispatch) and is the THINNEST consumer by design (thin-orchestrator rule §5.7 forbids large reads; orchestrator context is the scarcest resource). The orchestrator→operator summary is a decision-gating claim crossing the largest information-asymmetry boundary in the system (the operator \"is never handed code to read/write\" — playbook §1 — and cannot cheaply verify). The proposal's stops do not address the orchestrator-as-consumer edge or the orchestrator→operator edge. These are arguably THE dominant unguarded boundaries.\n- **Reviewer→Orchestrator edge (verdict consumption).** The reviewer produces a verdict that gates merge/dispatch; the orchestrator consumes it. The verdict is a decision-gating claim, but STOP B is framed as auditing Builder output, not reviewer verdicts. The review pipeline's Consolidate stage is an unguarded consumer edge under the proposal.\n- **Long-running-agent temporal drift.** A kickoff agent running 45m produces intermediate checkpoint comments; each is a claim crossing the agent→operator boundary. A \"tests pass\" claim at minute 5 may be stale by minute 40. The proposal's stops are point-in-time gates (post-plan, pre-consumption) and don't address temporal certainty decay. Should STOP B apply to every checkpoint (expensive, contradicts \"~4 max\") or only the final result (intermediate drift unguarded)? Unaddressed.\n\n### FINDING 5 (MAJOR) — \"Zero-cost byproduct\" overstates model-agreement collection\n\nThe proposal says agreement data is \"a zero-cost byproduct of the standing multi-model review workflow (#241).\" Testing this against #241 schema §6:\n\n- **§6.3 step 2 (taxonomy mapping) is non-trivial classification work.** Mapping each reviewer's free-text findings into the fixed C1-C10 taxonomy requires reading and interpreting every finding and deciding its class. The schema calls this \"structuring\" but it is classification + interpretation — new analytical work on top of the existing synthesis, not a reformatting of it.\n- **§6.3 step 3 (per-cell assignment) requires re-reading reviewer output in detail.** To distinguish `disagreed` (saw the claim and rejected it) from `not-found` (simply missed it), the recorder must carefully read the reviewer's full text — not just the verdict. This is additional reading the orchestrator would not otherwise do.\n- **The thin-orchestrator rule (§5.7) is violated or a hidden agent is required.** Agreement collection requires reading ALL reviewer findings in detail. If the orchestrator does this, it violates \"the orchestrator's context is the scarcest resource; large reads must be delegated.\" If a subagent does it, that is a new agent dispatch — not zero-cost. The schema §6.1 hedges with \"the orchestrator (or a designated recorder)\" — the \"designated recorder\" is a hidden new agent role not present in the current four-role model.\n- **Non-gating collection feeding operational routing is a hidden cost.** §6.2 says collection must not gate the flow and missing verdicts are marked, not waited on. But §8 says agreement statistics feed the Model Routing Matrix (routing decisions). So routing decisions are made on best-effort, possibly-incomplete agreement data. The committed routing matrix (5c92e9df) already integrated DRAFT agreement statistics + 3 routing rows — including lower-confidence RECONSTRUCTED back-fill rows influencing operational routing. Bad routing from incomplete/reconstructed agreement data is a hidden cost the \"zero-cost\" framing hides.\n\n**Accurate framing:** \"low-incremental-cost byproduct (no new review-wave dispatches) with a hidden classification tax and a recorder-role requirement.\" Not zero-cost.\n\n### FINDING 6 (MAJOR) — Principle 1a (\"what was NOT tested\") has no enforcement point\n\nPrinciple 1a requires claims to state \"what was NOT tested.\" This is excellent. But no stop is explicitly assigned to enforce it:\n- STOP A is a testability audit (does the plan ADMIT cheap tests?), not a \"what-was-not-tested declaration\" gate.\n- STOP B is a result audit — could check the declaration, but the proposal doesn't say so.\n- STOP C is re-verification, not declaration.\n\nThe principle-to-stop mapping is implicit. Without an explicit enforcement point, \"state what was not tested\" is an unenforced aspiration.\n\n### FINDING 7 (MINOR→MAJOR on adversarial scrutiny) — The information-asymmetry framing is one-directional and inverts at the review edge\n\nThe proposal's topology finding says the failure point is where \"producer can verify cheaply, thin consumer cannot,\" and the corrective is \"producer-side calibration.\" But at the REVIEW edge the inversion holds: the REVIEWER (consumer of the builder's work) can often verify cheaply by reading the code, while the BUILDER (producer) is the one who cannot objectively assess its own work. The information asymmetry at the review edge favors the CONSUMER, not the producer. This means \"producer-side calibration\" (STOP B) is backwards for the review edge — the consumer (reviewer) is already the strong party and the verifier. STOP B is redundant at the very edge the proposal presents as load-bearing. The one-directional framing doesn't hold across all edges.\n\n### ADVERSARIAL COUNTEREXAMPLES (concrete breaks)\n\n**C1 — The cheapest-test-first trilemma.** Principle 1b: \"assumptions that gate decisions must be tested with the quickest cheapest discriminating test.\" But the cheapest test is usually the one the producer already ran. If the producer ran it, the result is a claim crossing the boundary (needs certainty calibration). If the consumer re-runs it, that's not \"cheapest\" — it's redundant. If nobody runs it (because the producer claims it passed), the assumption is untested. Trilemma: cheap (trust the claim) vs. discriminating (consumer re-runs, not cheap) vs. untested (nobody runs it). The proposal doesn't resolve which horn.\n\n**C2 — The certainty-vocabulary infinite regress.** Principle 1a requires claims to state \"HOW CERTAIN (guess/evidence-based/proven).\" But the certainty label is ITSELF a claim crossing the boundary. Is the label \"evidence-based\"? What is the evidence for the label? To calibrate the certainty label you need a certainty label for the certainty label. \"Proven\" means \"I ran a test\" — but the test result is itself a claim crossing the boundary. The vocabulary pushes the regress one level; it doesn't break it. The proposal has no meta-certainty escape.\n\n**C3 — STOP A penalizes legitimately-expensive-to-test plans.** STOP A flags plans that don't admit cheap discriminating tests. But some correct plans are genuinely expensive to test (e.g. \"rewrite the hydration mechanism\" — the discriminating test is \"does the full system hydrate,\" which is L3). STOP A would flag this as \"untestable at L1\" — but the plan is correct, just expensive. This creates pressure to decompose until everything is L1-testable, biasing toward trivially-testable work over important-but-hard-to-test work. The proposal doesn't handle the \"legitimately expensive\" case.\n\n**C4 — Model-agreement collection assumes a prerequisite that isn't met.** The #241 schema is DRAFT, pending operator approval, with open questions (§9) on taxonomy granularity, verdict normalization, and storage form. The proposal treats agreement collection as \"zero-cost byproduct of the standing workflow\" — but the standing workflow doesn't yet produce standardized verdicts (the schema isn't canonical). The \"byproduct\" requires the schema to be adopted first. The proposal assumes a prerequisite that is unmet.\n\n**C5 — Contamination is an asymmetry violation in the opposite direction.** The §5.6 reviewer-independence incident (a reviewer read another's verdict) is an information-asymmetry failure, but in the OPPOSITE direction from the proposal's framing: the CONSUMER over-accessed producer-side information it shouldn't have. The proposal's framing is about producers under-calibrating consumers; contamination is about consumers over-accessing. The topology finding's one-directional framing doesn't capture this observed failure mode.\n\n**C6 — Silent-hang/stalled-agent failures are outside the framing.** #138 (flash 10.5h), #142 (10.9h), #129 (laguna) — the agent's status flag lies (says RUNNING while dead). This is a producer-side calibration failure (the system claims \"running\" with false certainty) but it is NOT a \"decision-gating claim crossing a role boundary\" — it is an infrastructure telemetry failure. The proposal's framing doesn't capture this dominant observed failure class in this repo's history.\n\n---\n\n### WHAT THE PROPOSAL GETS RIGHT (credit where due)\n\n- The **topology finding** (information-asymmetry boundary as the dominant unguarded failure point) is a genuine, novel, correct insight. It explains the §5.6 contamination incident, the #154 R1 masked divergence, and the B1 1-of-3 catch in a unified frame.\n- **Principle 1a** (certainty vocabulary including \"what was NOT tested\") is the right discipline. The \"what was NOT tested\" obligation specifically targets the cheapest class of false-confidence failure.\n- **Principle 1b** (cheapest-test-first) correctly inverts the default (agents tend to over-verify expensively or not verify at all; this forces the discriminating cheap test).\n- **STOP C scoping** (only high-stakes decisions) correctly avoids making everything expensive — the instinct to gate only install/merge is right, even though \"high-stakes\" is undefined.\n- **Model-agreement as a quality index** is the right idea — finding-level agreement catching masked divergence (the #154 R1 B2 example) is real and load-bearing.\n\n---\n\n### CONDITIONAL — required hardening before implementation\n\nThe proposal is **directionally correct** (the topology finding is right; the principles are adoptable) but **operationally incomplete**. To move from CONDITIONAL to PASS, the proposal must:\n\n1. **Define STOP B's performer** and resolve the self-audit paradox (Finding 1). This is the load-bearing gap.\n2. **Add a bounce-back budget + escalation path + bounded \"audit pass\" criterion** to STOP B (Finding 2). Apply principle 1a to the bounce-back channel itself.\n3. **Define L1/L2/L3 or explicitly map them to the existing `local`/`ci`/`thorough` verification tiers** (Finding 3). Stop calling an LLM reasoning audit \"L1 cheap\" without cost grounding.\n4. **Extend stop coverage to sentinel, swarm inter-phase edges, orchestrator-as-consumer, and orchestrator→operator edges** (Finding 4). The Builder→Reviewer edge is one of several; the orchestrator-as-consumer edge is arguably the dominant one and is unaddressed.\n5. **Correct the \"zero-cost byproduct\" claim** for model-agreement collection (Finding 5). Acknowledge the classification tax and the recorder-role requirement; decide whether the recorder is the orchestrator (violates thin-orchestrator) or a new agent dispatch (not zero-cost).\n6. **Assign an explicit enforcement point for principle 1a** (\"what was NOT tested\") (Finding 6).\n7. **Address the information-asymmetry inversion at the review edge** (Finding 7) — STOP B is redundant where the consumer is the strong party; the proposal needs a consumer-side-verification stop for the review edge, not a producer-side-calibration stop.\n8. **Resolve the cheapest-test trilemma and the certainty-vocabulary regress** (C1, C2) — at minimum, state which horn of each trilemma the proposal takes.\n\n**Verdict: CONDITIONAL.** Adopt the two principles now. Do not implement the stops as specified until the above are resolved — STOP B without a defined performer and without a bounce-back budget would create unbounded round-trips and, via the self-audit paradox, would either violate the no-self-review rule or add a hidden agent dispatch at every edge.","created_at":"2026-08-07T22:41:26.317322572Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:txtxRbCGncxPs5YVqhd0abUgfcruU3ze6o9USZ9e9ck","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgBrkJWbwQmXES5+ltWRxRKeJmS2PIO3BfyCG5CwZdt0oAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAsqGlsFJAYfnNFWwWrM9jAeQxtFyHV8usOllgB+Zw8g3TX8ESySzKlKM9Kz4mOXKVFCY2mvrSd5IEXX67o2bbDQ=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:txtxRbCGncxPs5YVqhd0abUgfcruU3ze6o9USZ9e9ck","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgBrkJWbwQmXES5+ltWRxRKeJmS2PIO3BfyCG5CwZdt0oAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQDcBtb5kG6Krsdpxrs8WV44ElSLNkuRHv7neVl0NvBcbuMPxTFc+bGgMABHaJm3LjrCcOGbQ09RPI1WpYdetGwQ="}
