{"agent_id":"OL2r","agent_seq":1547,"timestamp":"2026-08-09T13:51:06.321390072Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"1566dff5-d465-4b23-a4bb-3f12073a22df","title":"Triage opencode DB bloat: inventory 'Say hi.' flood + session-store split (fork vs main)","description":"POST-CRASH HOUSEWORK (session #23). CONTEXT: After the opencode TUI crash (load 5.6+, 9h TUI at 56% CPU, duplicate #412 kickoffs), operator restarted fresh and killed all orphans. Machine healthy (load 0.63). REMAINING: the /sessions list shows only the fork DB (opencode-fork-pp3g.db, 1.13GB, 104MB WAL) which is flooded with ~1,731 'Say hi.' hang-model test sessions from the #270/#273/#274 fork verification; the real history (41,488 sessions, 31,072 ASES hits) lives in main opencode.db (2.6GB) which the fork binary does not read — root cause of 'sessions gone' report. GOAL: (1) read-only inventory of both DBs — session counts by project, 'Say hi.'/hang-model prevalence, size breakdown; (2) produce a safe prune/vacuum plan (backup-then-prune, WAL checkpoint while binary idle) WITHOUT touching the live fork DB while a session runs; (3) recommend whether the fork should read opencode.db or keep the separate fork DB. EVIDENCE dirs: ~/.local/share/opencode/ (opencode.db, opencode-fork-pp3g.db, backups/, fork-backups/20260808-233133/). READ-ONLY audit — no deletes, no vacuum, no config changes. Deliverable: findings + plan on this issue. Ref: #270/#273/#274/#294/#156.","priority":"medium","created_by":"OL2r"},"signed_by":"SHA256:txtxRbCGncxPs5YVqhd0abUgfcruU3ze6o9USZ9e9ck","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgBrkJWbwQmXES5+ltWRxRKeJmS2PIO3BfyCG5CwZdt0oAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQKX/c9JA8vTu55qL/ln3vhkg63/vkhE5fdNK470RHkwxm88wAwtwAD1/ebRYz/ztc0akVWdZIlT1KAU68gEAKwI="}
